From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Grische <github@grische.xyz>
Date: Mon, 10 Aug 2026 09:20:09 +0000
Subject: net: ethernet: lantiq: add xRX500 central DMA controller driver
 (hdma)

Add the driver for the xRX500 central DMA engines. Four controller
instances serve the ethernet datapath (DMA1TX, DMA1RX, DMA2TX, DMA2RX).
Each is reset at postcore probe and configured from a table in the
driver, keyed by its register base, which carries its identity together
with its arbitration, burst length, polling interval, descriptor
placement, prefetch, byte enables, byte order and software poll quota.
None of that describes anything a board can wire differently. Port and
channel state is programmed after that, with port control 0x1F03,
channel control and class, and interrupt status cleared with interrupts
left disabled, because the CBM, not the CPU, services these channels in
steady state. Clocks are deliberately left untouched:
the boot chain hands over running clocks (see the clk driver's
CLK_IGNORE_UNUSED policy).

The controller identity is exported so that a driver holding a phandle
to a controller can name a channel on it. The compatible is spelled the
way the binding does.

The channel-level API (ltq_request_dma, ltq_dma_chan_on/off/reset,
descriptor allocation and window configuration, IRQ enable/disable) is
consumed by the CBM patches that follow. One detail learned on hardware
is embedded here:

 - Channel-config faithfulness: the vendor never zeroes
   CDBA/CDLEN/CDPTNR when (re)configuring a live channel, and the
   DMA interrupt-enable mask must keep the egress channel bit set;
   deviating from either stalls the egress path. The V3
   memory-window readback used by other SoCs aliases onto DMA_CTRL
   on GRX500 and must not be read.

Descriptor rings are bounded to [PHYS_OFFSET, PHYS_OFFSET + SZ_256M), at
both ends of the ring. This bound is a property of the uncached window,
not the top of DRAM.

The SegCtl this platform boots under maps kernel VA 0xA0000000-0xBFFFFFFF
uncached onto physical 0x20000000-0x3FFFFFFF, and only the lower half of
that is DRAM. The upper half -- VA 0xB0000000 and up -- is the SoC's
register space, appearing there at its nominal address plus 0x20000000,
and it is what every ioremap() on this platform is served out of. So the
uncached alias of DRAM is exactly the first 256 MiB of the window, and
above PHYS_OFFSET + SZ_256M a DRAM page's uncached alias *is* a register
address.

That band is not harmlessly empty, and bounding at the first block that
answers rather than at the window edge would not be conservative. A
coherent page at physical 0x31477000 aliases onto nominal 0x11477000,
nothing is decoded there, and a kernel-mode memcpy through it took a
fatal data bus error on this silicon. Higher up the alias decodes onto
live registers instead, where the write simply lands while the device
goes on reading the untouched DRAM page.

This driver keeps its own copy of the bound rather than relying on the
generic check in arch/mips/mm/dma-noncoherent.c, because it aliases
addresses by hand instead of using the pointer the allocator returned,
and so never passes through that check.

A ring's CPU view is derived from its physical address as
CKSEG1ADDR(desc_phys), and the pointer dma_free_coherent() is handed is
kept apart from it, so a channel that does not own its ring never frees
one.

hdma_port_chan_tbl() maps each dequeue port the buffer manager serves to
its transmit channels, ports 7 to 10 on DMA2TX for the LAN ports and
port 19 on DMA1TX for the WAN port. A port's channels are opened once
and stay open, because closing and reopening a CBM-managed channel
leaves the dequeue handshake stale, and a failed open releases every
channel it acquired.

Descriptor fetch-on-demand is switched on as the last step of controller
initialisation, after every port and channel is configured, so the
controller never fetches from a channel still in its reset state.

Nothing builds this code yet. The buffer manager, the traffic manager
and the DMA driver resolve symbols against each other, so they are wired
into the build together once the last of them exists.

Ported from the AVM FRITZ!Box 7560 GPL release (Linux 4.9.198),
drivers/dma/intel/hdma.c/.h and include/linux/dma/lantiq_dmax.h -
